import json
from pathlib import Path
p=Path('/workspace/sites/olympiad-atlas')
url='https://www.obm.org.br/content/uploads/2022/11/obm2022_nivel2.pdf'
new=[]
def add(num,difficulty,topic,tags,statement,solution,reason):
 new.append(dict(id=f'brazil-obm-level-2-2022-{num}',country='Brazil',competition='OBM Level 2',year=2022,number=str(num),statement=statement,solution=solution,sourceUrl=url,solutionUrl=url,solutionKind='original-exposition',topic=topic,tags=tags.split('|'),difficulty=difficulty,ratingReason=reason,reviewStatus='individually-assessed',notes='Original mathematical paraphrase and independently worked solution. Official Portuguese problem paper verifies provenance.'))
add(2,5,'Algebra','Systems of equations|Symmetric polynomials',r'Nonzero real numbers $a,b,c$ satisfy $a+ab=c$, $b+bc=a$, and $c+ca=b$. Determine all possible values of $abc$.',r'Put $s=a+b+c$ and $t=abc\ne0$. Summing the equations gives $ab+bc+ca=0$. Multiplying them and cancelling $abc$ gives $(1+a)(1+b)(1+c)=1$, hence $s+t=0$. Multiplying each equation by the missing variable also gives $3t=a^2+b^2+c^2=s^2=t^2$. Therefore $t=3$.'+'\n\n'+r'To verify existence, take a root $a\in(0,1)$ of $a^3+3a^2-3=0$ (the polynomial changes sign on this interval), and set $b=-(2a+3)/(a+1)$ and $c=-a(a+2)/(a+1)$. These are nonzero. Direct substitution verifies the three equations: the last two reduce to $a^3+3a^2-3=0$. Thus the unique possible value is $\boxed3$.','Several symmetric identities must be combined: sum the equations, multiply them, then multiply each by its missing variable. These force t squared=3t. Verifying existence, rather than merely deriving a necessary value, adds a final algebraic step.')
add(5,5.5,'Number theory','Divisibility|Reachability|Prime factors',r'Start with $2022$. At each step, replace the current integer $n$ by $n+d$, where $d>1$ is any positive divisor of $n$. Find the greatest composite integer that can never occur.',r'Every even integer at least $2022$ is reachable, by repeatedly adding $2$. For an odd composite integer $N$ with least prime divisor $p$, if $N-p\ge2022$, then $N-p$ is reachable and divisible by $p$, so adding $p$ reaches $N$.'+'\n\n'+r'For $N\ge2068$, the bound $p\le\sqrt N$ gives $N-p\ge2022$. The odd composites from $2035$ to $2067$ have the following least prime divisors: $2035:5$, $2037:3$, $2041:13$, $2043:3$, $2045:5$, $2047:23$, $2049:3$, $2051:7$, $2055:3$, $2057:11$, $2059:29$, $2061:3$, $2065:5$, $2067:3$. Each satisfies $N-p\ge2022$, so every composite greater than $2033$ is reachable.'+'\n\n'+r'Finally $2033=19\cdot107$. In a last move $n+d=2033$ with $d\mid n$, the divisor $d$ also divides $2033$. Thus $d\ge19$ and $n\le2014$, impossible since the sequence begins at $2022$ and increases. The answer is $\boxed{2033}$.','Work backwards from an odd target: subtracting its smallest prime factor produces a reachable even predecessor. A square-root bound reduces the global claim to a short finite check, while the obstruction at 2033 requires analysing every possible final divisor.')
add(6,6.5,'Combinatorics','Graph theory|Odd cycles|Set systems',r'What is the largest $k$ such that every family of $k$ distinct $1011$-element subsets of $\{1,2,\ldots,2023\}$ can be split into two collections, with every two sets in the same collection having a common element?',r'Write $m=1011$. Make a graph whose vertices are the chosen sets, joining two when they are disjoint. The required split is a bipartition. If $A,B,C$ form a two-edge path, then $A$ and $C$ are both $m$-element subsets of the $(m+1)$-element complement of $B$. Hence $|A\setminus C|\le1$.'+'\n\n'+r'In an odd cycle of length $2r+1$, follow the $2r$-edge path from a set $A$ to its other neighbour $D$. Each two-edge step changes at most one element, so $|A\setminus D|\le r$. But $A,D$ are disjoint, giving $r\ge m$. Every odd cycle therefore has at least $2m+1=2023$ vertices. A graph on at most $2022$ vertices has no odd cycle and is bipartite (colour each connected component by the parity of distance from a fixed vertex).'+'\n\n'+r'To show failure at $2023$, use labels modulo $2023$ and all cyclic translates $A_i=\{i,i+2,\ldots,i+2020\}$, for $i=0,\ldots,2022$. These sets are distinct and successive sets, including the last and first, are disjoint. They form an odd cycle, which cannot be bipartitioned. Thus the answer is $\boxed{2022}$.','The problem becomes a bipartiteness question on a disjointness graph. The essential observation is that a two-step walk changes at most one element; this bounds odd-cycle length and must be paired with an explicit sharp cyclic family.')

data=json.loads((p/'data/americas.json').read_text());ids={x['id'] for x in new}
data=[x for x in data if x['id'] not in ids]+new
